WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.807
SQL uses three-valued logic. A condition can be TRUE, FALSE, or UNKNOWN.

00:00:05.807 --> 00:00:10.122
Comparing a normal value with NULL gives UNKNOWN, not FALSE.

00:00:10.122 --> 00:00:12.417
And NOT UNKNOWN remains UNKNOWN.

00:00:12.417 --> 00:00:16.412
Because WHERE returns only rows whose condition is TRUE, UNKNOWN behaves like a filtered-out row.

00:00:16.412 --> 00:00:19.082
This explains the NOT IN behavior.

00:00:19.082 --> 00:00:27.587
Connect this idea to a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up.

00:00:27.587 --> 00:00:30.887
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:30.887 --> 00:00:39.467
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:44.752
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:44.752 --> 00:00:49.920
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
